[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186133719 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ \\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\,R\\phantom{|}}\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and $Q^T.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363154 :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x={Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410808 :question/problem "$$\nx=\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401097 :question/problem "$$\nx=\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\bbox[6px,border:1px solid black]{\\,\\phantom{|}\\,\\,{R}^{-1}\\,}\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186410809 :question/problem "$$\nx=\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\bbox[4px,border:1px solid black]{{R}^{-1}\\phantom{|}}\\,\\,{Q}^{T}\\,\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and $b.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e0061631-6269-58a0-a054-4e33a330ba6f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0061631-6269-58a0-a054-4e33a330ba6f" :question/id #uuid "e0061631-6269-58a0-a054-4e33a330ba6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139798" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,R=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99733e29-1d7e-5770-81f4-dc925a89eb32"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99733e29-1d7e-5770-81f4-dc925a89eb32" :answer-field/id #uuid "99733e29-1d7e-5770-81f4-dc925a89eb32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2e9e21c3-28d7-5744-b751-8735eef80c3e" "answer-6c912a39-aa9c-5584-bd27-ebb5f9b76c23" "answer-ae08b2e2-ed79-5464-95a9-3d40e4f6d24d" "answer-45ccbd17-a611-5e51-8f96-c134314902f7" "answer-8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e9e21c3-28d7-5744-b751-8735eef80c3e" :answer/id #uuid "2e9e21c3-28d7-5744-b751-8735eef80c3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c912a39-aa9c-5584-bd27-ebb5f9b76c23" :answer/id #uuid "6c912a39-aa9c-5584-bd27-ebb5f9b76c23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae08b2e2-ed79-5464-95a9-3d40e4f6d24d" :answer/id #uuid "ae08b2e2-ed79-5464-95a9-3d40e4f6d24d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45ccbd17-a611-5e51-8f96-c134314902f7" :answer/id #uuid "45ccbd17-a611-5e51-8f96-c134314902f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84" :answer/id #uuid "8c06bd3e-d522-582e-8e6e-842930f9ae84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135143 :tutorial/content "Let's find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where $A = QR,$ and\n![](images/4e/4e2433312f3a424aa161d5aeec63df38787c43435a9c2af44ba6b3d63f9ef065.svg)\n\nPreviously, we showed that the normal equation corresponding to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where $A=QR,$ can be reduced to\n\n$$\nR \\mathbf{x} = Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our matrices into this equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4(2) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =10 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/72/723835f5ecb612e7d79c29ada9cc05241ffd4f233e29c9ed9a643994580a8c36.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186133719 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ \\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\,R\\phantom{|}}\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and $Q^T.$"] [17592186363154 :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x={Q}^{T}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?"] [17592186410808 :question/problem "$$\nx=\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186401097 :question/problem "$$\nx=\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[5px,border:1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?"] [17592186401097 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{align